      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to color each of the six hexagons using the colors red (R), yellow (Y), or green (G) such that no two adjacent hexagons share the same color. We start with the hexagon with center R (where R is colored red) and then color each of the adjacent hexagons.\n\nHere is the reasoning step-by-step:\n\n1. The right side of the hexagon has two vertices already colored red (the right side has two adjacent vertices on either side of the center vertex and only one of these contains R), so the right side must be colored with either yellow (Y) or green (G).\n2. Let's say we choose to color the right terminal side with Y. The right vertex hexagon must therefore be Y. Similarly, the hexagon directly above the Y must be G, and the hexagon directly to the right of this G will be Y, etc.\n3. The left side hexagon is going to be colored red (the line connecting R to Y will not have a new color from the existing R as we will move to the left). The third hexagon from the left, which is directly connecting to the center hexagon, must take a color which has not yet appeared (either Y or G), but the Y on the right side is already used, so this hexagon must be G, etc. This pattern repeats and if we find all possible combinations, we can count them.\n\nTo achieve this systematically, we can build a recursion or follow a trail of logic.\n\nHere's how we do it programmatically (using Python for easy counting and logic checking).\n\nThe shortest code in Python can be used to simulate and check all possible colorings.\n\nI've created a Python code snippet to simulate this color-painting and color-checking process.\n```python\nfrom itertools import product\nfrom functools import cache\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# Define a set of steps that have to be applied to hexagon at each color-to-be-coded position\nsteps = [\n    # Leftmost hexagon\n    { 'diff1': 1, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': 0 },    # Red to Green\n    { 'diff1': 0, 'diff2': 1, 'diff3': 0 },    # Red to Yellow\n    { 'diff1': 0, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': 1 },    # Red to Green\n    # Through the central hexagon at two diff1 positions\n    { 'diff1': 1, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': 1 },    # Red to Yellow\n    { 'diff1': 0, 'diff2': 1, 'diff3': 1 },    # Red to Green\n    { 'diff1': 1, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': 0 },    # Red to Green\n    # Rightmost hexagon\n    { 'diff1': -1, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': 1 },    # Red to Yellow\n    { 'diff1': 0, 'diff2': -1, 'diff3': 0 },    # Red to Green\n    { 'diff1': 1, 'diff2': 0, 'diff3': -1 },    # Red to Yellow\n]\n\n# Every resulting form of color is chosen in such a way,\n# the starting vertex is red.\ncolor_status = defaultdict(lambda: 'red')\ncolor_arrived = []\n\n# Semantic encoding of vertices, but didomans have to be implemented.\n# Each vertex color is recorded and checked against hashing before color changes\n# Doomed to shorten this otherwise lengthy looking algorithm\ncolor_and_check = []\n\ncolor_to_replace_by = {'Y': 'red', 'G': 'red', 'R': 'red'}\nfor start_color in ['R', 'G']: # Doomed for long recursive algo, changed once\n  color_status[start_color] = start_color\n  visited = {start_color}\n  stack = [[], []] # Placeholders for some significances\n  current_color_visited = start_color\n  print('gary start point: ', start_color)\n#endchilddue to long algo gary end point ....\n\n  while current_color_visited:\n    next_color = color_to_replace_by[str(color_status[current_color_visited])]\n    print('gary coloring now ', next_color)\n    if next_color in color_arrived:\n      # There must be conflict if histogram repeats <last took color\n      break\n    else:\n      color_status[current_color_visited] = next_color\n      color_arrived.append(next_color)\n      stack[current_color_visited][1].add(next_color)\n      current_color_visited = next_color\n#endfor\n# Condition which if no more steps, added to the path\nwhile len(COLOR_STACK) > 0:\n  print('PAYING DUDE')\n  current_color = list(COLOR_STACK).pop()\n  COLOR_HISTORY[current_color].append(COLOR_STACK) \n  COLOR_STACK[current_color].clear()\n  print('winning duende')\n\n\n# Print obtained results for HEXAGON coloring\nprint('estedest..')\n\n\n# Test potential results from simulation and check they are red-green-yellow\nfor stack in COLOR_STACK.values():\n  print(' Checking NOT red-green-yellow color definition')\n  alle = []\n  for n in stack:\n    alle.append(n)\n  print('ALL ', 'ALL ', alle)\n```",
