      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "There are three choices for the first hexagon (the red one).\nThere are two choices for each of the other five hexagons, since coloring them must be different colors than the hexagon to their left or right. Since the hexagon to the right and left of the red hexagon are neighboring hexagons, that hexagon cannot be colored red. This means the other hexagons have two choices each, rather than three.\nThere are $3!$ ways to color the other hexagons.\nSince there are $3$ ways to color the red hexagon and $2$ ways to color each of the other six hexagons, the count of ways that work is $(3)(2^6) = \\boxed{192}$.\nThe answer is: 192",
